# =============================================================================
# Vesopa-Ltd — the umbrella repository
# =============================================================================
# This repo carries every project in one place. The rules below are in three
# groups, and the first group is the one that matters:
#
#   1. SECRETS      — things that must never leave this machine
#   2. Build output — things that are regenerated, not authored
#   3. Noise        — editor and OS droppings
#
# A secret committed once is public for ever, even if the next commit removes
# it: it stays in the history, in every clone, and in GitHub's cache. Rotating
# the credential is then the only fix. Check `git status` before the first push.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. SECRETS  —  never commit
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every .env variant, whatever it is called and wherever it lives. The pattern
# is deliberately broad: `.env.production`, `.env.local`, `.env.staging.bak`
# and anything else somebody invents on a Friday are all caught.
.env
.env.*
*/.env
*/.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# ...except the templates, which are the whole point of having templates.
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
!**/.env.example
!**/.env.*.example

# App signing keys and certificates.
#
# The Android keystores in vesopa_epos are the single most dangerous files in
# this tree. A leaked signing key lets anyone publish an app that Android will
# accept as a genuine Vesopa update, and it CANNOT be rotated — every device
# with the app installed is pinned to that key for the life of the app.
*.key
*.keystore
*.jks
*.p12
*.pfx
*.pem
*.cer
*.mobileprovision
key.properties
keystore.properties
**/keys/
google-services.json
GoogleService-Info.plist
*credentials*.json
*service-account*.json

# SSH and cloud credentials, in case one is ever dropped in here.
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
.ssh/
.aws/
.npmrc
.pypirc


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Build output and dependencies  —  regenerated, never authored
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
package-lock.json.bak
.pnpm-store/

# Flutter / Dart (vesopa_epos)
.dart_tool/
.packages
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
build/
*.iml
.pub-cache/
.pub/

# Gradle and Android.
# `.gradle/` alone is 27MB of caches, and `executionHistory.bin` inside it
# changes on every single build — committing it would put a 27MB binary diff in
# the history every time anybody compiled.
.gradle/
local.properties
captures/
*.apk
*.aab
*.aar

# iOS / macOS
Pods/
*.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
*.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
DerivedData/

# Windows / Linux desktop builds
**/windows/flutter/ephemeral/
**/linux/flutter/ephemeral/

# Node / web build output
dist/
.next/
.nuxt/
.cache/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Assets kept out of git deliberately
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 125MB of source artwork — layered masters, not the exported files the sites
# actually serve. Git stores every version of a binary in full, so a repo is the
# wrong home for these; they belong in Drive or object storage. The exported PNG
# and SVG the apps use are already committed inside each project.
logo and assets/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Logs, databases and scratch
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
logs/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pm2.log
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.sql.gz
dump.rdb
tmp/
temp/
.scratch/


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Editor and OS noise
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.history/

# 125MB of layered brand masters, same reasoning as "logo and assets/":
# source artwork belongs in asset storage, not in every clone of the repo.
brandAssets/
brandAssets.zip
brand_assets/
p.md
# Credential notes kept alongside the repo. Never committed — the filename is
# an accurate description of what is in it, and this repo is on GitHub.
ClientAllCredentialsForAllBusinessWeHaveWithExplanation.md
*Credentials*.md

# The client conversation that specified the loyalty programme. Private
# correspondence rather than project documentation, and it has already
# served its purpose as the spec — it stays on this machine.
Conversation I had with my client regarding this.pdf
